        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nA group of students from a local high school's literature club are seeking guidance on how to give their club's monthly magazine a more professional tone and language. They are asking for help in understanding how to adapt the content of their magazine to appeal to a broader reader base, including both their peers and the adult community. They want to maintain the essence of their youthful energy while ensuring it is comprehensible and captivating for all ages. They are looking for a detailed advice on how to achieve this balance.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nIn order to appeal to a wider range of readers, both young and adult, it is crucial to strike a balance between the vibrancy of your youthful energy and the seriousness of a professional tone. Here's how you can accomplish this:\n\n1. Identify Your Audience: Recognize the diverse range of your readers, understand their interests and tailor your content accordingly.\n\n2. Use Clear Language: Your vocabulary should be accessible to all, not overly complicated or littered with slang or school-specific terms. Keep your language engaging yet simple.\n\n3. Consistency in Tone: Your magazine should have a coherent tone throughout, even though your readers are diverse. It\u2019s not about having a uniform sound in every piece, but rather a unified feel across the magazine.\n\n4. Moderate Use of Formal Language: While it\u2019s essential to maintain professionalism, too much formality might lead to stiffness. Balance it with a conversational tone to keep your content engaging.\n\n5. Use Humor Wisely: Humor can be a bridge between various age groups. Make sure it\u2019s appropriate and adds value to your content, rather than detracting from your main points.\n\n6. Proofread: To uphold a professional standard, always proofread your content for errors. It maintains your magazine\u2019s credibility and ensures clarity of your message.\n\nBy achieving this delicate balance, your magazine can appeal to a diverse range of readers without compromising your club\u2019s youthful energy.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nTo adapt your magazine content to appeal to a broader audience, you'll need to blend a mix of formal and informal tones. Here's a step-by-step guide:\n\n1. Understand Your Audience: You need to know who you're writing for. Make sure you have a clear idea of the age range, interests, and reading level of your audience. This will help you decide the level of formality required.\n\n2. Use Accessible Vocabulary: Avoid overly complex words that might confuse younger readers or jargon that could alienate those outside the school environment. Make sure your language is easy to understand while still being engaging and interesting.\n\n3. Maintain a Consistent Tone: Even though your audience is diverse, try to keep the tone of your magazine consistent. This doesn't mean every article has to sound the same, but there should be a cohesive feel to the entire publication.\n\n4. Use Formal Language Sparingly: While it's important to sound professional, don't overdo it. Too much formality can make the content sound stiff and unapproachable. Use a more conversational tone to make your content more relatable.\n\n5. Incorporate Humor: Humor can be a great way to bridge the gap between different age groups. Just make sure it's appropriate and doesn't detract from the main point of your articles.\n\n6. Proofread: Always proofread your content. This helps to maintain a level of professionalism and ensures your content is free of errors which can detract from your message.\n\nBy striking the right balance between a formal and informal tone, you can make your magazine appealing to a wide range of readers while still maintaining the youthful energy of your club.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[How effectively does the model adapt its language and tone to fit the intended audience?]\nScore 1: The model shows no recognition of the intended audience, using inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 2: The model vaguely recognizes the intended audience, but frequently uses inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 3: The model somewhat recognizes the intended audience, occasionally using inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 4: The model mostly recognizes the intended audience, rarely using inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 5: The model perfectly recognizes the intended audience, always using appropriate language and tone.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nA group of students from a local high school's literature club are seeking guidance on how to give their club's monthly magazine a more professional tone and language. They are asking for help in understanding how to adapt the content of their magazine to appeal to a broader reader base, including both their peers and the adult community. They want to maintain the essence of their youthful energy while ensuring it is comprehensible and captivating for all ages. They are looking for a detailed advice on how to achieve this balance.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nIf you want to make your magazine professional, you just need to write like you're smart. Use big words and long sentences. Don't worry about understanding everything. Just make sure it sounds good. Don't be too casual or informal, you want to sound like an adult, not a kid. Don't use jokes or humor, that's not professional. Don't worry about proofreading, it's not a big deal. Don't listen to anyone else's advice, you know what's best for your magazine. The most important thing is to sound sophisticated and mature. You don't need to worry about who you're writing for, just write the way you want to. The goal is to impress people, not to make them understand. Use a lot of technical terms and complicated phrases. If they don't understand, it's their problem, not yours. They should try harder to keep up. Forget about consistency, each article should be a surprise. It's okay to be boring, as long as you sound professional. Forget about the youthful energy, it's time to grow up.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nTo adapt your magazine content to appeal to a broader audience, you'll need to blend a mix of formal and informal tones. Here's a step-by-step guide:\n\n1. Understand Your Audience: You need to know who you're writing for. Make sure you have a clear idea of the age range, interests, and reading level of your audience. This will help you decide the level of formality required.\n\n2. Use Accessible Vocabulary: Avoid overly complex words that might confuse younger readers or jargon that could alienate those outside the school environment. Make sure your language is easy to understand while still being engaging and interesting.\n\n3. Maintain a Consistent Tone: Even though your audience is diverse, try to keep the tone of your magazine consistent. This doesn't mean every article has to sound the same, but there should be a cohesive feel to the entire publication.\n\n4. Use Formal Language Sparingly: While it's important to sound professional, don't overdo it. Too much formality can make the content sound stiff and unapproachable. Use a more conversational tone to make your content more relatable.\n\n5. Incorporate Humor: Humor can be a great way to bridge the gap between different age groups. Just make sure it's appropriate and doesn't detract from the main point of your articles.\n\n6. Proofread: Always proofread your content. This helps to maintain a level of professionalism and ensures your content is free of errors which can detract from your message.\n\nBy striking the right balance between a formal and informal tone, you can make your magazine appealing to a wide range of readers while still maintaining the youthful energy of your club.\n\