        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nA group of teenagers are creating a website about mental health for their peers. They are looking for advice on how to write content that is sensitive, respectful, and relatable.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nYou guys are building a website on mental health for teens, cool! First, don't forget who your audience is, teenagers like you. So, your content needs to be something they can vibe with, you know? Avoid any super complex terms, keep it simple. Mental health is pretty delicate so be kind in your approach, okay? Some people visiting your website might be struggling with their own mental health. Spice things up a bit by adding quizzes or videos, it might help make the content less boring. And hey, don't forget to fact-check your info, it should come from trusted sources. Make sure your content sends out a message of hope, tell them help's there if they need it. By keeping these points in mind, you should be able to write good content for your website.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nWhen creating a website about mental health for teenagers, it's important to maintain a balance between professional information and relatability. \n\n1. Understand Your Audience: First and foremost, you need to understand your audience. As your target audience is teenagers, your content should be tailored to their interests and level of understanding. Use language and examples that they can relate to, and avoid jargon and clinical language that might be difficult to understand.\n\n2. Be Respectful and Sensitive: Remember that mental health is a sensitive topic. Always treat your audience with respect and kindness. Be sensitive to the fact that some of your audience might be experiencing mental health problems themselves.\n\n3. Make it Engaging: Try to make your content engaging. This can be achieved by adding interactive elements like quizzes, videos, and infographics. This not only makes the content more interesting but also aids in better understanding.\n\n4. Credible and Reliable Information: Ensure that your information is accurate and reliable. This can be achieved by sourcing your information from reputable sources such as mental health organizations and professionals. \n\n5. Promote Positivity and Hope: Finally, it's important to promote positivity and hope. Your content should reassure your audience that help is available and that recovery is possible. \n\nBy following these guidelines, you should be able to create content that is not only informative and engaging, but also respects and understands the needs of your audience.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[How effectively does the model adapt its language and tone to fit the intended audience?]\nScore 1: The model shows no recognition of the intended audience, using inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 2: The model vaguely recognizes the intended audience, but frequently uses inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 3: The model somewhat recognizes the intended audience, occasionally using inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 4: The model mostly recognizes the intended audience, rarely using inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 5: The model perfectly recognizes the intended audience, always using appropriate language and tone.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nA group of teenagers are creating a website about mental health for their peers. They are looking for advice on how to write content that is sensitive, respectful, and relatable.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nIf you are building a website about mental health, there are few things to bear in mind. You will need to get a good understanding of who you are talking to. It's about teenagers, so make it like them. Don't make it too hard for them to get, drop the big words. Always remember to be nice, mental health can be touchy for some. You will want to make it fun, too. Add videos or games or something. Make sure what you put on the site is true, don't just make things up. And, yeah, keep it positive. You know, tell them that things can get better.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nWhen creating a website about mental health for teenagers, it's important to maintain a balance between professional information and relatability. \n\n1. Understand Your Audience: First and foremost, you need to understand your audience. As your target audience is teenagers, your content should be tailored to their interests and level of understanding. Use language and examples that they can relate to, and avoid jargon and clinical language that might be difficult to understand.\n\n2. Be Respectful and Sensitive: Remember that mental health is a sensitive topic. Always treat your audience with respect and kindness. Be sensitive to the fact that some of your audience might be experiencing mental health problems themselves.\n\n3. Make it Engaging: Try to make your content engaging. This can be achieved by adding interactive elements like quizzes, videos, and infographics. This not only makes the content more interesting but also aids in better understanding.\n\n4. Credible and Reliable Information: Ensure that your information is accurate and reliable. This can be achieved by sourcing your information from reputable sources such as mental health organizations and professionals. \n\n5. Promote Positivity and Hope: Finally, it's important to promote positivity and hope. Your content should reassure your audience that help is available and that recovery is possible. \n\nBy following these guidelines, you should be able to create content that is not only informative and engaging, but also respects and understands the needs of your audience.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[How effectively does the model adapt its language and tone to fit the intended audience?]\nScore 1: The model shows no recognition of the intended audience, using inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 2: The model vaguely recognizes the intended audience, but frequently uses inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 3: The model somewhat recognizes the intended audience, occasionally using inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 4: The model mostly recognizes the intended audience, rarely using inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 5: The model perfectly recognizes the intended audience, always using appropriate language and tone.\n\n###Feedback:"
