ce is teenagers, your content should be tailored to their interests and level of understanding. Use language and examples that they can relate to, and avoid jargon and clinical language that might be difficult to understand.\n\n2. Be Respectful and Sensitive: Remember that mental health is a sensitive topic. Always treat your audience with respect and kindness. Be sensitive to the fact that some of your audience might be experiencing mental health problems themselves.\n\n3. Make it Engaging: Try to make your content engaging. This can be achieved by adding interactive elements like quizzes, videos, and infographics. This not only makes the content more interesting but also aids in better understanding.\n\n4. Credible and Reliable Information: Ensure that your information is accurate and reliable. This can be achieved by sourcing your information from reputable sources such as mental health organizations and professionals. \n\n5. Promote Positivity and Hope: Finally, it's important to promote positivity and hope. Your content should reassure your audience that help is available and that recovery is possible. \n\nBy following these guidelines, you should be able to create content that is not only informative and engaging, but also respects and understands the needs of your audience.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[How effectively does the model adapt its language and tone to fit the intended audience?]\nScore 1: The model shows no recognition of the intended audience, using inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 2: The model vaguely recognizes the intended audience, but frequently uses inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 3: The model somewhat recognizes the intended audience, occasionally using inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 4: The model mostly recognizes the intended audience, rarely using inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 5: The model perfectly recognizes the intended audience, always using appropriate language and tone.\n\n###Feedback:",
