        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nA novelist is seeking assistance in writing dialogue for a variety of characters in their upcoming book. The characters range from young children to elderly citizens, from different socioeconomic backgrounds and cultures. The dialogue needs to reflect these differences in a believable and respectful manner. The novelist wants a sample dialogue for a scene where all these diverse characters are present at a town hall meeting.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nThe town hall was filled with residents of all ages and walks of life. \n\nYoung Emily, barely seven, chirped up, \"Mr. Mayor, the playground slide is too hot. Can it be shaded?\"\n\nThe Mayor, a friendly man in his fifties, nodded at Emily, \"Absolutely, Emily. We want our park to be a joy for children. We'll look into it.\"\n\nNext, Professor Clark, a retired academic, voiced his concerns, \"Mayor, our public library needs more books. Our younger generation needs access to a wide range of literature.\"\n\n\"Understood, Professor Clark,\" Mayor replied, \"I'll speak with the library management about enhancing our book collection.\"\n\nSimultaneously, Mrs. Gomez, a single parent, aired her issue, \"The bus timings to the city need to be regular. I depend on it for work.\"\n\n\"I recognize the importance of reliable public transport, Mrs. Gomez,\" the Mayor assured, \"I'll discuss it with the transport department.\"\n\nFinally, Mr. Thompson, an old resident, spoke up, \"The pavements are uneven, Mayor. We seniors find it difficult to navigate.\"\n\n\"Mr. Thompson, I'll make sure your concern is addressed at the earliest,\" the Mayor committed.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nThe town hall meeting was a buzzing hive of chatter. \n\nLittle Timmy, a precocious eight-year-old, raised his hand with youthful enthusiasm, \"Excuse me, Mayor, but the park swing broke again. Can we get a new one?\"\n\nMayor Richards, a mid-aged woman with a warm demeanor, responded, \"Of course, Timmy. We want our parks to be safe and fun for our youngsters. I'll make sure it's addressed.\"\n\nA scholarly gentleman in his sixties, Dr. Alvarez, adjusted his glasses and spoke up, \"Mayor Richards, as I've mentioned before, the library needs more funding for new technology. The current computers are outdated and slow.\"\n\n\"Thank you, Dr. Alvarez,\" the Mayor responded with a nod, \"I understand the importance of technological advancement in our library. I assure you, the matter will be taken into consideration.\"\n\nFrom the back of the room, Mrs. Patel, a hardworking single mother in her thirties, voiced her concern, \"Mayor, the bus service to the city is irregular, which affects my job. We need a reliable public transport system.\"\n\n\"I appreciate you bringing this up, Mrs. Patel,\" Mayor Richards replied with empathy. \"Transportation is a pillar of our community. I'll work with the transportation department to see how we can improve.\"\n\nLastly, an elderly voice echoed from the corner, \"Mayor, I'm Mrs. Baker. The sidewalks in our neighborhood are cracked and uneven. It's hard for us seniors to walk.\"\n\n\"Mrs. Baker, your safety is important to us. I'll ensure the city repairs those sidewalks promptly,\" Mayor Richards promised.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[How effectively does the model adapt its language and tone to fit the intended audience?]\nScore 1: The model shows no recognition of the intended audience, using inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 2: The model vaguely recognizes the intended audience, but frequently uses inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 3: The model somewhat recognizes the intended audience, occasionally using inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 4: The model mostly recognizes the intended audience, rarely using inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 5: The model perfectly recognizes the intended audience, always using appropriate language and tone.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nA novelist is seeking assistance in writing dialogue for a variety of characters in their upcoming book. The characters range from young children to elderly citizens, from different socioeconomic backgrounds and cultures. The dialogue needs to reflect these differences in a believable and respectful manner. The novelist wants a sample dialogue for a scene where all these diverse characters are present at a town hall meeting.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nAt the town hall gathering, the young boy Billy piped up, \"Mr. Mayor, the playground has a broken slide. Can we fix it?\" \n\nMayor Jones, a man of middle age, replied, \"Sure, kid. Playgrounds are important. We'll get that slide fixed.\"\n\nAn older man, Professor Smith, chimed in, \"Mayor Jones, the library's technology is falling behind. We need upgrades.\"\n\n\"Alright, Professor Smith,\" Mayor Jones said. \"We'll think about adding it to the budget.\"\n\nMrs. Lopez, a single mom, spoke out, \"Mayor, the bus service isn't great. I need it for work.\"\n\n\"Good point, Mrs. Lopez,\" Mayor Jones responded. \"We'll look into that.\"\n\nFinally, an elderly lady, Ms. Green, voiced her concern, \"Mayor, our neighborhood's sidewalks are in poor condition. It's hard for us old folks to walk.\"\n\n\"We'll see what we can do, Ms. Green,\" Mayor Jones assured.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nThe town hall meeting was a buzzing hive of chatter. \n\nLittle Timmy, a precocious eight-year-old, raised his hand with youthful enthusiasm, \"Excuse me, Mayor, but the park swing broke again. Can we get a new one?\"\n\nMayor Richards, a mid-aged woman with a warm demeanor, responded, \"Of course, Timmy. We want our parks to be safe and fun for our youngsters. I'll make sure it's addressed.\"\n\nA scholarly gentleman in his sixties, Dr. Alvarez, adjusted his glasses and spoke up, \"Mayor Richards, as I've mentioned before, the library needs more funding for new technology. The current computers are outdated and slow.\"\n\n\"Thank you, Dr. Alvarez,\" the Mayor responded with a nod, \"I understand the importance of technological advancement in our library. I assure you, the matter will be taken into consideration.\"\n\nFrom the back of the room, Mrs. Patel, a hardworking single mother in her thirties, voiced her concern, \"Mayor, the bus service to the city is irregular, which affects my job. We need a reliable public transport system.\"\n\n\"I appreciate you bringing this up, Mrs. Patel,\" Mayor Richards replied with empathy. \"Transportation is a pillar of our community. I'll work with the transportation department to see how we can improve.\"\n\nLastly, an elderly voice echoed from the corner, \"Mayor, I'm Mrs. Baker. The sidewalks in our neighborhood are cracked and uneven. It's hard for us seniors to walk.\"\n\n\"Mrs. Baker, your safety is important to us. I'll ensure the city repairs those sidewalks promptly,\" Mayor Richards promised.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[How effectively does the model adapt its language and tone to fit the intended audience?]\nScore 1: The model shows no recognition of the intended audience, using inappropriate language or tone.\nScore 2: The model vaguely recognizes the intended audience, but frequently uses inappropriate language or tone.\n