        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nA novelist is trying to write dialogue for a character in his new book. The character is a scientist who often has to explain complex concepts to non-scientists in a way they can understand. The novelist wants to write a scene where the scientist is explaining the concept of quantum mechanics to a high school student.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nThe scientist, with a twinkle in his eye and an infectious enthusiasm, started to explain the profound mysteries of quantum mechanics to the eager high school student. \"Alright,\" he began, using a simple analogy, \"let's think about your phone. It can be on and off, right? However, in the realm of quantum mechanics, your phone could be in a state of being both on and off at the same time, until you decide to check it.\"\n\nNoticing a flicker of uncertainty on the student's face, he shifted gears, tailoring his explanation. \"Let's consider it like your favorite song playlist. You know how it shuffles songs, playing them in a random order each time? In quantum mechanics, particles behave similarly. They can exist in multiple positions at the same time, like your song list ready to play any song.\"\n\nHe paused, allowing the student to digest this unconventional thought. \"When we observe these particles, similar to when your playlist decides to play a specific song, that's when they decide to be in one position.\"\n\nThe scientist finished, his tone gentle, understanding, and devoid of any superiority. \"Quantum mechanics can be quite a mental gymnastics. Don't worry if it feels hard to grasp. Remember, even those at the top of their fields are still puzzled and intrigued by it.\"\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nThe scientist took a deep breath, gathering his thoughts before speaking. He understood the importance of breaking down the complex world of quantum mechanics into digestible chunks, something a high schooler could grasp without feeling overwhelmed. \n\n\"Think of it like this,\" he began, choosing his words carefully. \"Imagine you're flipping a coin. Normally, you'd expect the coin to either land on heads or tails, right? But in quantum mechanics, it's as though the coin is both heads and tails at the same time, until you actually look at it.\u201d\n\nSeeing the student\u2019s brows furrow in confusion, the scientist continued, adapting his explanation to make it more relatable. \"Or, consider your favorite video game. You know how you can save your game, then try different strategies, and if it doesn't work, you can go back to the saved game and try something else? Quantum mechanics is similar. Particles can exist in multiple states at the same time, like your game character trying all the strategies simultaneously.\"\n\nHe paused, giving the student time to absorb this information. \u201cAnd it's only when we measure these particles, like when you choose one strategy to go with in your game, that these multiple possibilities collapse into one outcome.\u201d \n\nThe scientist ended his explanation with a warm smile, his tone encouraging, patient, and utterly devoid of any condescension. \"I know it can be hard to wrap your head around these concepts. It's okay to take your time to understand. After all, even the best minds in the world are still trying to fully understand quantum mechanics.\"\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the response demonstrate the ability to adapt language and tone based on the user's input (e.g., formal vs. informal, technical vs. non-technical)?]\nScore 1: The response does not adapt language or tone to the user's input, resulting in communication that may feel out of place or inappropriate.\nScore 2: The response shows limited ability to adapt language or tone, occasionally matching the user's input but often missing the mark.\nScore 3: The response demonstrates some ability to adapt language and tone, aligning with the user's input more often than not.\nScore 4: The response often adapts language and tone to the user's input, creating a natural and appropriate dialogue.\nScore 5: The response consistently adapts language and tone based on the user's input, seamlessly matching the communication style and creating a comfortable user experience.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nA novelist is trying to write dialogue for a character in his new book. The character is a scientist who often has to explain complex concepts to non-scientists in a way they can understand. The novelist wants to write a scene where the scientist is explaining the concept of quantum mechanics to a high school student.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nThe scientist started speaking in a complicated, jargon-heavy manner, \"Quantum mechanics refers to a fundamental theory in physics that provides a description of the physical properties of nature at the scale of atoms and subatomic particles. It is the foundation of all quantum physics including quantum chemistry, quantum field theory, quantum technology, and quantum information science. The superposition principle of quantum mechanics proposes that systems can exist in multiple states simultaneously, and this is known as a superposition of states. When observed or measured, the wave function collapses and the system is found to be in one of its eigenstates. Now, you should have understood it perfectly.\"\n\nHe did not pause, or attempt to gauge the student's understanding of the matter. He simply went on, stating, \"The dual nature of particles, the probability wave, wave-particle duality, the Heisenberg uncertainty principle, and quantum entanglement might be difficult concepts for a novice but are indispensable to comprehend quantum mechanics.\"\n\nThe scientist concluded, his tone heavy and emotionless, \u201cUnderstanding quantum mechanics may take time and a lot of effort. It\u2019s something even seasoned physicists find challenging. But remember, knowledge is power. So don't give up.\"\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nThe scientist took a deep breath, gathering his thoughts before speaking. He understood the importance of breaking down the complex world of quantum mechanics into digestible chunks, something a high schooler could grasp without feeling overwhelmed. \n\n\"Think of it like this,\" he began, choosing his words carefully. \"Imagine you're flipping a coin. Normally, you'd expect the coin to either land on heads or tails, right? But in quantum mechanics, it's as though the coin is both heads and tails at the same time, until you actually look at it.\u201d\n\nSeeing the student\u2019s brows furrow in confusion, the scientist continued, adapting his explanation to make it more relatable. \"Or, consider your favorite video game. You know how you can save your game, then try different strategies, and if it doesn't work, you can go back to the saved game and try something else? Quantum mechanics is similar. Particles can exist in multiple states at the same time, like your game character trying all the strategies simultaneously.\"\n\nHe paused, giving the student time to absorb this information. \u201cAnd it's only when we measure these particles, like when you choose one strategy to go with in your game, that these multiple possibilities collapse into one outcome.\u201d \n\nThe scientist ended his explanation with a warm smile, his tone encouraging, patient, and utterly devoid of any condescension. \"I know it can be hard to wrap your head around these concepts. It's okay to take your time to understand. After all, even the best minds in the world are still trying to fully understand quantum mechanics.\"\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the response demonstrate the ability to adapt language and tone based on the user's input (e.g., formal vs. informal, technical vs. non-technical)?]\nScore 1: The response does not adapt language or tone to the user's input, resulting in communication that may feel out of place or inappropriate.\nScore 2: The response shows limited ability to adapt language or tone, occasionally matching the user's input but often missing the mark.\nScore 3: The response demonstrates some ability to adapt language and tone, aligning with the user's input more often than not.\nScore 4: The response often adapts language and tone to the user's input, creating a natural and appropriate dialogue.\nScore 5: The response consistently adapts language and tone based on the user's input, seamlessly matching the communication style and creating a comfortable user experience.\n\n###Feedback:"
