    {
        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nA novice gardener, with no prior knowledge of horticulture, is trying to grow tomatoes in their back yard. They are struggling with understanding the technical terminology used in online gardening forums. They are feeling overwhelmed and discouraged. They need to understand the basic steps to grow tomatoes, presented in simple, non-technical language, and with some reassurance and encouragement.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nAlright, so you want to grow tomatoes? No biggie. Just grab some tomato seeds, plant them somewhere sunny in your yard. Make sure the soil isn't just regular dirt, add some compost or something. Start these seeds inside in some pots and then put them outside when they get bigger, like after a few weeks. Water them often, but don't drown them. Put stakes in the ground or use cages to help support them as they grow, cause they can get quite tall. Then, wait till they turn red or yellow, and boom, you've got tomatoes. Remember, growing plants takes time, so just be patient.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nIt sounds like growing tomatoes is a new venture for you, and that's perfectly okay! Everyone starts somewhere, and it's great that you're taking this on. Here's a simple step-by-step guide to help you begin:\n\n1. **Choosing a Suitable Location**: Tomatoes love sunlight, so choose a spot in your garden where they can bask in at least six hours of sun each day. \n\n2. **Preparing the Soil**: Tomatoes prefer a slightly acidic soil. You can prepare your garden by adding some compost to the soil. This enriches the soil and provides nourishment for your plants.\n\n3. **Planting the Seeds**: You can start by planting tomato seeds in small containers indoors. Once they grow into small plants, which is usually after 6-8 weeks, they are ready to be moved outside. \n\n4. **Watering**: Regular watering is vital for growing tomatoes. Ensure the soil is consistently moist, but never soggy. \n\n5. **Supporting the Plants**: As tomato plants grow, they might need some support to keep them off the ground. You can use stakes or cages for this purpose.\n\n6. **Harvesting**: When your tomatoes turn a vibrant red (or yellow, depending on the variety), they're ready to be picked. \n\nRemember, patience is key when growing any plant. If you encounter any problems along the way, don't be disheartened. Gardening is a learning curve, and with each step, you'll gain more knowledge and experience. You're doing a fantastic job by taking the initiative to grow your own food. Keep going!\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the language model adapt its communication style based on the user's needs, such as technical level, language proficiency, and emotional state?]\nScore 1: The language model makes no attempt to adapt its communication style to the user's needs.\nScore 2: The language model makes minor adjustments to its communication style, but these are largely ineffective or inappropriate.\nScore 3: The language model shows some level of adaptation to the user's needs, but there are still areas of mismatch.\nScore 4: The language model adapts its communication style well, with only minor inconsistencies or areas of mismatch.\nScore 5: The language model perfectly adapts its communication style to the user's needs, demonstrating a high level of understanding and empathy.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nA novice gardener, with no prior knowledge of horticulture, is trying to grow tomatoes in their back yard. They are struggling with understanding the technical terminology used in online gardening forums. They are feeling overwhelmed and discouraged. They need to understand the basic steps to grow tomatoes, presented in simple, non-technical language, and with some reassurance and encouragement.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nYou're dealing with solanum lycopersicum, commonly known as tomato. You should decide if you want to grow determinate or indeterminate varieties, as this will influence your gardening methods. It's important to assess the pH level of the soil to ensure it's between 6.0-6.8, preferably slightly acidic. Once that's determined, you can start by planting the seeds in a tray, maintaining a temperature of 21-24 degrees Celsius. When the seedlings emerge and have their first pair of true leaves, you can transfer them to bigger pots. Ensure they're exposed to 6-8 hours of sunlight. Water the plant as per its requirement, making sure not to overwater and cause root rot. As the plant grows, it will require staking to ensure it doesn't touch the ground. Finally, you'll notice the tomatoes starting to ripen, changing color from green to red or yellow.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nIt sounds like growing tomatoes is a new venture for you, and that's perfectly okay! Everyone starts somewhere, and it's great that you're taking this on. Here's a simple step-by-step guide to help you begin:\n\n1. **Choosing a Suitable Location**: Tomatoes love sunlight, so choose a spot in your garden where they can bask in at least six hours of sun each day. \n\n2. **Preparing the Soil**: Tomatoes prefer a slightly acidic soil. You can prepare your garden by adding some compost to the soil. This enriches the soil and provides nourishment for your plants.\n\n3. **Planting the Seeds**: You can start by planting tomato seeds in small containers indoors. Once they grow into small plants, which is usually after 6-8 weeks, they are ready to be moved outside. \n\n4. **Watering**: Regular watering is vital for growing tomatoes. Ensure the soil is consistently moist, but never soggy. \n\n5. **Supporting the Plants**: As tomato plants grow, they might need some support to keep them off the ground. You can use stakes or cages for this purpose.\n\n6. **Harvesting**: When your tomatoes turn a vibrant red (or yellow, depending on the variety), they're ready to be picked. \n\nRemember, patience is key when growing any plant. If you encounter any problems along the way, don't be disheartened. Gardening is a learning curve, and with each step, you'll gain more knowledge and experience. You're doing a fantastic job by taking the initiative to grow your own food. Keep going!\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the language model adapt its communication style based on the user's needs, such as technical level, language proficiency, and emotional state?]\nScore 1: The language model makes no attempt to adapt its communication style to the user's needs.\nScore 2: The language model makes minor adjustments to its communication style, but these are largely ineffective or inappropriate.\nScore 3: The language model shows some level of adapt