ages can offer the support they need.\n\n6. **Pick your tomatoes**: When your tomatoes turn bright red or yellow, it's time to enjoy the fruits of your labor.\n\nDon't worry if things don't go perfectly the first time. Gardening is a learning experience and you're doing an amazing job just by trying. Keep up the good work!\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nIt sounds like growing tomatoes is a new venture for you, and that's perfectly okay! Everyone starts somewhere, and it's great that you're taking this on. Here's a simple step-by-step guide to help you begin:\n\n1. **Choosing a Suitable Location**: Tomatoes love sunlight, so choose a spot in your garden where they can bask in at least six hours of sun each day. \n\n2. **Preparing the Soil**: Tomatoes prefer a slightly acidic soil. You can prepare your garden by adding some compost to the soil. This enriches the soil and provides nourishment for your plants.\n\n3. **Planting the Seeds**: You can start by planting tomato seeds in small containers indoors. Once they grow into small plants, which is usually after 6-8 weeks, they are ready to be moved outside. \n\n4. **Watering**: Regular watering is vital for growing tomatoes. Ensure the soil is consistently moist, but never soggy. \n\n5. **Supporting the Plants**: As tomato plants grow, they might need some support to keep them off the ground. You can use stakes or cages for this purpose.\n\n6. **Harvesting**: When your tomatoes turn a vibrant red (or yellow, depending on the variety), they're ready to be picked. \n\nRemember, patience is key when growing any plant. If you encounter any problems along the way, don't be disheartened. Gardening is a learning curve, and with each step, you'll gain more knowledge and experience. You're doing a fantastic job by taking the initiative to grow your own food. Keep going!\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the language model adapt its communication style based on the user's needs, such as technical level, language proficiency, and e