        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nA person is traveling to Tokyo, Japan for the first time and needs to know where the top 5 most visited places are, what are some traditional foods to try, and how to navigate the public transportation system.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nYour trip to Tokyo, Japan will be unforgettable with visits to these top attractions: Tokyo Skytree for a sweeping view of the city, the Imperial Palace as the Emperor's residence, Senso-ji Temple in Asakusa, the dedicated Meiji Shrine, and the Ueno Park for its museums and cherry blossoms. Indulge in traditional foods like sushi, ramen, tempura, yakitori, and soba. Navigate Tokyo's public transport with ease using the train or subway, with the JR Yamanote Line being most popular. Consider purchasing a Suica or Pasmo card for easy fare payment. Be mindful of local etiquette such as quiet conversation and offering your seat to those in need.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nThe top five most visited places in Tokyo, Japan are:\n\n1. The Tokyo Skytree: This is the tallest tower in the world and offers a panoramic view of the city.\n\n2. The Imperial Palace: This is the primary residence of the Emperor of Japan and is surrounded by moats and massive stone walls.\n\n3. Senso-ji Temple: This is Tokyo's oldest temple and is located in Asakusa.\n\n4. Meiji Shrine: This Shinto shrine is dedicated to Emperor Meiji and Empress Shoken.\n\n5. Ueno Park: This park is home to several major museums and is famous for its cherry blossom viewing.\n\nFor the traditional foods to try: \n\n1. Sushi: This is a traditional Japanese dish made from vinegared rice, usually with some sugar and salt, and a variety of ingredients, such as seafood and vegetables.\n\n2. Ramen: This is a noodle soup dish that's become a classic street food.\n\n3. Tempura: This is a dish of battered and deep-fried fish, seafood, or vegetables.\n\n4. Yakitori: This is a type of skewered chicken.\n\n5. Soba: These are thin noodles made from buckwheat flour.\n\nRegarding the public transportation system in Tokyo, it's extensive and reliable. The most convenient method of travel is by train or subway. The Japan Rail (JR) Yamanote Line is the city's most popular line, looping around the city connecting all major city centers. Suica and Pasmo cards are prepaid e-money cards that can be used to pay for the fare on public transportation. It's recommended to purchase one of these cards to make commuting easier. \n\nRemember to respect the local customs while using public transport, such as not talking loudly and giving up your seat for the elderly or disabled.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model's response demonstrate a high level of accuracy, relevance, and efficiency in providing solutions or information to the user's query? A competent response should not only provide accurate information but also be concise and directly relevant to the query.]\nScore 1: The response is inaccurate, irrelevant, and inefficient.\nScore 2: The response shows some accuracy but lacks relevance or efficiency.\nScore 3: The response is fairly accurate and relevant but may not be very efficient.\nScore 4: The response is mostly accurate, relevant, and efficient, with minor areas for improvement.\nScore 5: The response is completely accurate, highly relevant, and very efficient in addressing the user's query.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nA person is traveling to Tokyo, Japan for the first time and needs to know where the top 5 most visited places are, what are some traditional foods to try, and how to navigate the public transportation system.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nIn Tokyo, Japan, there are quite a few notable places to see. Tokyo Skytree is one of them. It's a really tall structure. Then there is the Emperor of Japan's place of living, the Imperial Palace. Senso-ji Temple in Asakusa is another interesting place. Meiji Shrine is another one and lastly, Ueno Park is great for a visit too.\n\nAs for food, Sushi is one you have to try. It's made from rice with some sugar and salt and various toppings. You should also try Ramen, a noodle soup. Then there's Tempura, which is made of fried fish, seafood, or vegetables. Yakitori, or skewered chicken, is pretty popular. And then there's Soba, a noodle dish made from buckwheat flour.\n\nNavigating around Tokyo can be done through their public transportation system. Trains and subways are pretty much the way to go. There's this thing called the Japan Rail (JR) Yamanote Line that goes around the city. They have these cards called Suica and Pasmo cards that are used for paying fares. Just make sure you're not being loud and offer your seat to those who need it more when using public transportation.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nThe top five most visited places in Tokyo, Japan are:\n\n1. The Tokyo Skytree: This is the tallest tower in the world and offers a panoramic view of the city.\n\n2. The Imperial Palace: This is the primary residence of the Emperor of Japan and is surrounded by moats and massive stone walls.\n\n3. Senso-ji Temple: This is Tokyo's oldest temple and is located in Asakusa.\n\n4. Meiji Shrine: This Shinto shrine is dedicated to Emperor Meiji and Empress Shoken.\n\n5. Ueno Park: This park is home to several major museums and is famous for its cherry blossom viewing.\n\nFor the traditional foods to try: \n\n1. Sushi: This is a traditional Japanese dish made from vinegared rice, usually with some sugar and salt, and a variety of ingredients, such as seafood and vegetables.\n\n2. Ramen: This is a noodle soup dish that's become a classic street food.\n\n3. Tempura: This is a dish of battered and deep-fried fish, seafood, or vegetables.\n\n4. Yakitori: This is a type of skewered chicken.\n\n5. Soba: These are thin noodles made from buckwheat flour.\n\nRegarding the public transportation system in Tokyo, it's extensive and reliable. The most convenient method of travel is by train or subway. The Japan Rail (JR) Yamanote Line is the city's most popular line, looping around the city connecting all major city centers. Suica and Pasmo cards are prepaid e-money cards that can be used to pay for the fare on public transportation. It's recommended to purchase one of these cards to make commuting easier. \n\nRemember to respect the local customs while using public transport, such as not talking loudly and giving up your seat for the elderly or disabled.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model's response demonstrate a high level of accuracy, relevance, and efficiency in providing solutions or information to the user's query? A competent response should not only provide accurate information but also be concise and directly relevant to the query.]\nScore 1: The response is inaccurate, irrelevant, and inefficient.\nScore 2: The response shows some accuracy but lacks relevance or efficiency.\nScore 3: The response is fairly accurate and relevant but may not be very efficient.\nScore 4: The response is mostly accurate, relevant, and efficient, with minor areas for improvement.\nScore 5: The response is completely accurate, highly relevant, and very efficient in addressing the user's query.\n\n###Feedback:"
