        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nA person with no medical background is trying to understand the reasons behind the rise in cases of antibiotic resistance in recent years. Could you explain the causes, implications, and potential solutions for this issue in a way that a non-medical person can understand?\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nAntibiotic resistance is when bacteria change and become stronger due to the use of antibiotics. Overuse and wrong use of these medicines are major factors contributing to this problem. When people use antibiotics too often, especially for diseases they can't treat like viral infections, bacteria learn how to resist the drugs that are supposed to kill them. The same thing happens when antibiotics are taken without doctor's advice. \n\nAnother reason is the inadequate management of infection in places such as hospitals. Sometimes, hospitals don't have good sanitation or don't enforce regular vaccination, and this can lead to antibiotic-resistant bacteria spreading. Also, the development of new antibiotics is slow and expensive, which makes it less attractive for companies to work on.\n\nAntibiotic resistance can have serious consequences. It can make it harder to cure diseases, which means more time spent in hospital, more money spent on treatment, and a higher chance of death. It's also more dangerous for people with weak immune systems, like older people or those with long-term health conditions.\n\nTo solve this issue, we need to be more careful about how we use antibiotics. We should only use them when necessary and always follow the doctor's instructions. In hospitals, better sanitation and regular vaccination can help to prevent the spread of resistant bacteria. Finally, more funding for research into new antibiotics is necessary. Governments, companies and research organizations need to work together on this.\n\nTo summarize, antibiotic resistance is a serious problem caused by many different factors. It can have big effects on people's health and the economy. However, with the right strategies, such as responsible use of antibiotics, better hospital practices, and more research into new antibiotics, we can handle this issue.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nAntibiotic resistance is a significant global health concern. It occurs when bacteria change in response to the use of antibiotics, rendering them ineffective. The causes of antibiotic resistance are multifaceted.\n\nFirstly, the overuse and misuse of antibiotics significantly contribute to this problem. When antibiotics are frequently used or used inappropriately, bacteria can adapt and develop resistance to the drugs designed to kill them. This includes situations where antibiotics are used to treat viral infections against which they have no effect, or when they are taken without medical supervision.\n\nSecondly, poor infection control practices, such as inadequate sanitation or lack of routine vaccinations, can contribute to the spread of antibiotic-resistant bacteria. In healthcare settings, this can result in hospital-acquired infections that are difficult to treat.\n\nLastly, the inadequate development of new antibiotics also plays a role. The process of developing new drugs is costly and time-consuming, which can deter pharmaceutical 