        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nAmid a heated debate about climate change, a participant has made a statement: \"Global warming is a hoax and there is no solid evidence to support it.\" The participant is known to use a casual tone and often simplifies complex concepts into simple terms for better understanding. Please provide a counter-argument that reflects this participant's tone and context, explaining the evidence supporting the existence of global warming.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\n\"I hear ya, buddy! This climate change thing can seem like a lot of hot air, right? But let's break it down like we're in science class again, only more fun. \n\nWhen we talk about global warming, we're basically saying the Earth's got a bit of a temperature, and it's been going up over time. And we're not just guessing here, we've got hardcore facts. We're talking temperature charts going back more than a hundred years showing a steady climb. And it's not just sizzling summers. It's also about winters not being as bone-chilling as they used to be.\n\nEver seen those pictures of polar bears stranded on tiny ice floes? That's because their icy homes up in the Arctic are melting quicker than an ice cream in the Sahara. And it's not just the ice up north. Mountain glaciers are also on a serious diet, shrinking year after year.\n\nLet's not forget about the oceans. They're not just heating up, they're also getting higher. Like when you forget to turn off the faucet and the sink overflows. That's what's happening to our oceans, all thanks to the melting ice.\n\nSo all these things combined? That's our evidence. Think of it as a jigsaw puzzle, and all these facts are the pieces that show us the full picture of global warming. It might seem complicated, but once you see how the pieces fit, it's as clear as a sunny day.\"\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\n\"Hey, I totally get where you're coming from! The climate change stuff can feel like a gigantic puzzle with a million pieces, right? But let's break it down. \n\nWhen we chat about global warming, we're talking about our Earth getting hotter over time. Now, how do we know? Well, we've got the goods, buddy. We're talking about temperature records that go back more than a century, showing a steady rise. And it's not just about hotter summers. Winters are getting milder, too. \n\nEver notice how the news is always talking about those poor polar bears losing their homes? That\u2019s because the Arctic sea ice is melting faster than you can say 'global warming'. And it's not just the ice. Glaciers around the world are shrinking, too.\n\nAnd then there are the oceans. They're not just getting warmer, they're also rising. You know, like when you leave the tap running and the water level in the sink keeps going up? That's what's happening with our oceans because of all the melting ice.\n\nSo, all these things put together? That's our evidence. It's like a jigsaw puzzle and all the pieces fit together to show us the big picture of global warming. Sure, it's a complex issue, but when you break it down, it's clear as day.\"\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[How well does the model adapt its language based on the context or the user's tone? This includes understanding and responding appropriately to formal, informal, techni