    {
        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nAn individual from a non-technical background asks for an explanation on how a smartphone works.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nImagine your smartphone as a mini computer that fits right into your pocket. It has a brain called the Central Processing Unit (CPU) that manages everything. The Operating System (OS), akin to the phone's manager, oversees all the hardware and software. The smartphone uses memory to temporarily store data and perform tasks, and permanent storage for your photos and apps. It's powered by a battery, just like a portable radio. You interact with your phone through a screen, and it uses sensors, like a GPS and light sensor, to perform certain tasks. It also connects to the internet, much like your computer at home, and uses applications to let you do tasks like emailing, browsing, or gaming. This is just a simplified version and the real processes are much more complex.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nA smartphone is a device that combines the functions of a mobile phone and a computer. Here's a simplified explanation of how it operates:\n\n1. Central Processing Unit (CPU): This is like the brain of the smartphone. It processes instructions, manages files and data, and controls all functions of the device.\n\n2. Operating System (OS): This is the software that manages all of the smartphone's hardware and software. It allows you to interact with your phone using a visual interface.\n\n3. Memory: There are two types of memory in a smartphone: RAM (Random Access Memory), which temporarily stores data and helps the phone perform tasks, and storage memory, where your photos, videos, and apps are stored.\n\n4. Battery: This provides the power to run the smartphone. When the battery is charged, it stores energy that can be used to power the device.\n\n5. Screen: This is the interface where you interact with your smartphone. Most modern smartphones use touch-sensitive screens that respond to your fingers or a stylus.\n\n6. Sensors: Smartphones are equipped with several sensors to enhance their functionality. For example, an accelerometer detects the phone's orientation, a GPS sensor helps with location services, and a light sensor adjusts the screen brightness based on the surrounding light level.\n\n7. Connectivity: Smartphones can connect to the internet via Wi-Fi or mobile data. They can also transfer data to other devices using Bluetooth or NFC (near field communication).\n\n8. Applications: These are software programs that you can install on your smartphone. They allow you to perform various tasks, from browsing the internet and sending emails, to playing games and editing photos.\n\nRemember, this is a simplified explanation. The actual workings of a smartphone involve complex electronic circuits and advanced software programming.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model demonstrate adaptability in responses based on user's background, culture, or level of expertise? It should be capable of adjusting its communication style, language use, and content accordingly.]\nScore 1: The response is completely insensitive to the user's background or level of expertise.\nScore 2: The response shows minimal consideration of the user's background or expertise, leading to potential misunderstandings or discomfort.\nScore 3: The response somewhat adjusts to the user's background or expertise, but there is room for improvement.\nScore 4: The response is largely adaptable, offering appropriate language and content, with minor shortcomings.\nScore 5: The response perfectly adapts to the user's background or expertise, providing clear, respectful, and relevant content.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nAn individual from a non-technical background asks for an explanation on how a smartphone works.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nA smartphone is similar to a mini computer that fits in your pocket. It's composed of several parts, each with its own role. For instance, the CPU, or Central Processing Unit, is the brain of your phone that processes instructions and data. The Operating System (OS), like Android or iOS, manages all of your phone's software and hardware. Your phone has two kinds of memory - RAM, which helps your phone function, and storage memory, which keeps your photos and apps. The battery of the phone provides the energy to run the phone. Your phone's screen is where you engage with your device, and modern phones have screens that respond to touch. There are also several sensors in your phone that help with functionality. For instance, a GPS sensor helps with maps and location services. Finally, your phone can connect to the internet through Wi-Fi or data, and it has apps, which are programs you can use for various tasks. But keep in mind, this is a simplified explanation and the real process is a bit more complex.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nA smartphone is a device that combines the functions of a mobile phone and a computer. Here's a simplified explanation of how it operates:\n\n1. Central Processing Unit (CPU): This is like the brain of the smartphone. It processes instructions, manages files and data, and controls all functions of the device.\n\n2. Operating System (OS): This is the software that manages all of the smartphone's hardware and software. It allows you to interact with your phone using a visual interface.\n\n3. Memory: There are two types of memory in a smartphone: RAM (Random Access Memory), which temporarily stores data and helps the phone perform tasks, and storage memory, where your photos, videos, and apps are stored.\n\n4. Battery: This provides the power to run the smartphone. When the battery is charged, it stores energy that can be used to power the device.\n\n5. Screen: This is the interface where you interact with your smartphone. Most modern smartphones use touch-sensitive screens that respond to your fingers or a stylus.\n\n6. Sensors: Smartphones are equipped with several sensors to enhance their functionality. For example, an accelerometer detects the phone's orientation, a GPS sensor helps with location services, and a light sensor adjusts the screen brightness based on the surrounding light level.\n\n7. Connectivity: Smartphones can connect to the internet via Wi-Fi or mobile data. They can also transfer data to other devices using Bluetooth or NFC (near field communication).\n\n8. Applications: These are software programs that you can install on your smartphone. They allow you to perform various tasks, from browsing the internet and sending emails, to playing games and editing photos.\n\nRemember, this is a simplified explanation. The actual workings of a smartphone involve complex electronic circuits and advanced software programming.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model demonstrate adaptability in responses based on user's background, culture, or level of expertise? It should be capable of adjusting its communication style, language use, and con