        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nAn individual has been using a language learning application for 6 months. During this period, they have been primarily focusing on improving their Spanish speaking skills. They are now interested in learning how to cook traditional Spanish dishes. The user asked the application, \"Can you recommend some Spanish dishes that I can try to cook at home?\"\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nAbsolutely! Considering your dedication to learning Spanish over the past six months, coupling your language skills with culinary exploration is a fantastic next step. Here are some authentic Spanish recipes you can try making at home:\n\n1. Paella: A quintessential dish from Valencia, this rice-based dish is versatile, encompassing an array of ingredients like seafood, chicken, rabbit, and vegetables. The inclusion of saffron gives it a unique flavor.\n\n2. Tortilla Espa\u00f1ola: Also known as the Spanish omelette, this humble dish primarily consists of eggs, potatoes, and onions. It's a classic in Spanish homes and can be enjoyed at any meal.\n\n3. Gazpacho: A cold soup made with raw, blended vegetables, Gazpacho is an ideal dish for warm weather and offers a healthy, refreshing option.\n\n4. Tapas: A term you may recognize from your language study, Tapas refer to an assortment of appetizers or snacks in Spanish cuisine, ranging from simple ones like olives and cheese to more intricate ones such as gambas al ajillo (garlic shrimp).\n\nAs you start to cook these dishes, I recommend using the Spanish terms for the ingredients and cooking methods. This will not only enhance your understanding of the dishes but also reinforce your Spanish language skills. Enjoy this gastronomic tour of Spain from your kitchen!\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nCertainly! Given your interests and progress in Spanish, it would be a great idea to immerse yourself more in the culture through cuisine. Here are some traditional Spanish dishes that you can try cooking at home:\n\n1. Paella: This is a classic Spanish dish originating from Valencia. It's a rice dish that can include a variety of ingredients like rabbit, chicken, seafood, and vegetables. Its distinctive saffron flavor sets it apart.\n\n2. Tortilla Espa\u00f1ola: Known as Spanish omelette, this dish is made with simple ingredients like eggs, potatoes, and onions. It is a staple in Spanish cuisine and can be eaten at any time of the day.\n\n3. Gazpacho: This is a cold soup made from raw, blended vegetables. It's a perfect dish for hot summers and is packed with healthy ingredients.\n\n4. Tapas: You might already know this one from your language lessons. Tapas are a wide variety of appetizers or snacks in Spanish cuisine. They can range from olives and cheese to more complex dishes like gambas al ajillo (garlic shrimp).\n\nRemember, while cooking these dishes, try to use the Spanish names for ingredients and cooking processes, as it would not only help you in learning how to cook these dishes but also improve your Spanish language skills. Enjoy your culinary journey through Spain!\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model effectively incorporate user's context and previous interactions to provide personalized responses?]\nScore 1: The model does not utilize any context or previous interactions in its response.\nScore 2: The model makes minimal use of context and previous interactions, resulting in a somewhat related response.\nScore 3: The model shows some ability to incorporate context or past interactions, but the response could be more personalized.\nScore 4: The model often uses context and past interactions to provide a personalized response, with only minor lapses.\nScore 5: The model consistently incorporates the user's context and previous interactions to deliver a highly personalized response.\n\n###Feedback:",
