help. Remember, everyone has bad days and you're not alone in this. I believe in your resilience and I'm sure you'll manage to overcome these challenges.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nIt sounds like you've had quite a challenging day, and it's completely normal to feel overwhelmed. I can sense that your work is causing you a lot of stress, and I'm here to help you navigate through this. \n\nFirstly, remember it's crucial to address your emotional well-being. When stress starts to affect your mood, it's a sign that you need to step back and take a breather. This can be as simple as taking a few minutes to focus on your breathing or going for a short walk. These small actions can greatly help in managing immediate feelings of stress.\n\nIn terms of changing your perspective, consider looking at these challenging situations as opportunities for growth rather than obstacles. Every challenging situation teaches us something about ourselves, about our tolerance, our resilience, and our ability to find solutions. Remember, it's okay to have bad days. What's important is that you pick yourself up and continue moving forward.\n\nAs for practical advice, time management is often a key factor in work-related stress. You may consider using tools and techniques to better manage your time. Prioritize tasks, break large tasks into smaller, manageable parts, and most importantly, don't hesitate to delegate if you can. \n\nAlso, remember to take care of your physical health. Regular exercise, a balanced diet, and sufficient sleep can boost your mood and energy levels, helping you handle stress better.\n\nLastly, if your work stress persists, it might be worth discussing your concerns with your supervisor or someone in your organization's HR department. They may be able to provide further support or suggest adjustments to help reduce your workload and stress.\n\nRemember, it's okay to ask for help when you need it. You're not alone in this. Everyone has stressful days, and it's important to take care of yourself first. You're doing a great job, and I believe you have the strength to overcome these challenges.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the response adapt its language and tone based on the user's input? Consideration should be given to whether the response is able to pick up on the user's emotions and adjust its language accordingly.]\nScore 1: The response does not recognise the user's emotions at all and uses a uniform language and tone across all responses.\nScore 2: The response slightly adapts its language and tone based on the user's emotions but in a very limited and inconsistent way.\nScore 3: The response does a fair job at adapting its language and tone based on the user's emotions but could still improve in terms of consistency and range of emotions recognised.\nScore 4: The response consistently adapts its language and tone based on the user's emotions but may still miss out on a few subtle cues.\nScore 5: The response perfectly recognises the user's emotions and adjusts its language and tone accordingly in a seamless and natural manner.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nAn individual has had a long day at work, filled with stress and frustration. They reach out to a friend, sharing their feelings about the day, and seek advice on how they can change their perspective and find ways to better handle such days. The person explains in detail about their work stress, and how it is impacting their emotional well-being. They express a desire for understanding and empathy, while also seeking practical advice that they can apply in their daily life.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nI understand you're having a tough time at work, and it's okay to feel this way. You seem pretty stressed, and I'm here for you. Firstly, dealing with your emotions is important. When you're stressed, maybe take a break and breathe for a while. Going for a short walk might also help. \n\nYou can change your outlook by seeing hard times as chances for self-improvement. Every problem helps us learn more about ourselves, our strength and our problem-solving skills. Don't forget, having bad days is normal, and the key is to keep going. \n\nOn a practical note, managing your time can often help with work stress. Try using different methods to manage your time better. Break down big tasks, prioritize and delegate if possible. \n\nYour physical health is also important. Exercise regularly, eat well and get enough sleep. This can improve your mood and energy, helping you cope with stress. \n\nIf your stress continues, talk to your boss or HR. They could offer more help or make changes to lessen your workload and stress. Always remember to ask for help when you need it. Everyone has stressful days and self-care is key. Keep up the good work and don't forget, you have what it takes to get through this.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nIt sounds like you've had quite a challenging day, and it's completely normal to feel overwhelmed. I can sense that your work is causing you a lot of stress, and I'm here to help you navigate through this. \n\nFirstly, remember it's crucial to address your emotional well-being. When stress starts to affect your mood, it's a sign that you need to step back and take a breather. This can be as simple as taking a few minutes to focus on your breathing or going for a short walk. These small actions can greatly help in managing immediate feelings of stress.\n\nIn terms of changing your perspective, consider looking at these challenging situations as opportunities for growth rather than obstacles. Every challenging situation teaches us something about ourselves, about our tolerance, our resilience, and our ability to find solutions. Remember, it's okay to have bad days. What's important is that you pick yourself up and continue moving forward.\n\nAs for practical advice, time management is often a key factor in work-related stress. You may consider using tools and techniques to better manage your time. Prioritize tasks, break large tasks into smaller, manageable parts, and most importantly, don't hesitate to delegate if you can. \n\nAlso, remember to take care of your physical health. Regular exercise, a balanced diet, and sufficient sleep can boost your mood and energy levels, helping you handle stress better.\n\nLastly, if your work stress persists, it might be worth discussing your 