, and it's completely normal to feel overwhelmed. I can sense that your work is causing you a lot of stress, and I'm here to help you navigate through this. \n\nFirstly, remember it's crucial to address your emotional well-being. When stress starts to affect your mood, it's a sign that you need to step back and take a breather. This can be as simple as taking a few minutes to focus on your breathing or going for a short walk. These small actions can greatly help in managing immediate feelings of stress.\n\nIn terms of changing your perspective, consider looking at these challenging situations as opportunities for growth rather than obstacles. Every challenging situation teaches us something about ourselves, about our tolerance, our resilience, and our ability to find solutions. Remember, it's okay to have bad days. What's important is that you pick yourself up and continue moving forward.\n\nAs for practical advice, time management is often a key factor in work-related stress. You may consider using tools and techniques to better manage your time. Prioritize tasks, break large tasks into smaller, manageable parts, and most importantly, don't hesitate to delegate if you can. \n\nAlso, remember to take care of your physical health. Regular exercise, a balanced diet, and sufficient sleep can boost your mood and energy levels, helping you handle stress better.\n\nLastly, if your work stress persists, it might be worth discussing your concerns with your supervisor or someone in your organization's HR department. They may be able to provide further support or suggest adjustments to help reduce your workload and stress.\n\nRemember, it's okay to ask for help when you need it. You're not alone in this. Everyone has stressful days, and it's important to take care of yourself first. You're doing a great job, and I believe you have the strength to overcome these challenges.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the response adapt its language and tone based on the user's input? Consider