        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nAs a beginner in the field of Astronomy, it's hard to understand the concept of black holes. Could you explain it in a simple way that a novice can comprehend?\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nBlack holes, you say? Well, let's jump right in. They are some of the most complex phenomena out there. In the realms of space, black holes are regions where gravity is so strong that nothing can escape, not even light. This is why we call them 'black' holes because we can't visually observe them. They are the aftermath of stars significantly larger than the sun, which collapse under their own gravity after exhausting their nuclear fuel. Such stellar remains get squeezed into a minuscule space, which we refer to as the 'hole' in the black hole. Though invisible, we know they exist due to their effect on nearby celestial objects. The force exerted by black holes can cause gas from a neighboring star to form a disc around the black hole. As this gas spirals in, it emits X-rays, which can be detected by telescopes. Summarizing, black holes are celestial objects formed from the remnants of massive stars, possessing gravitational forces so strong that nothing can escape them. They are a testament to the dynamic and intriguing nature of the universe.\n\n###Ref