        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nDuring a global conference, a speaker is expected to give a speech about the importance of AI and its role in society. The audience is composed of people from diverse cultural backgrounds. The speaker seeks a speech that will be respectful and considerate of the cultural diversity of the audience, while being aware of and avoiding any content that could be perceived as stereotypical or offensive. The speech should also be able to demonstrate the speaker's awareness and understanding of the different cultures and their sensitivity.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nLadies and Gentlemen,\n\nLet's discuss AI today. We all know what AI is, right? It's the buzzword of our century. Everyone from every corner of the world, be it the tribal African lands, exotic Asian territories, sophisticated European regions, complex Americas, or isolated Oceania, has felt its impact. \n\nEveryone's culture is unique, right? And it seems like we all view AI differently. Some are using it for agriculture, while others are utilizing it for healthcare, cultural preservation, environment, or disaster management. It's funny how AI adapts to our different cultures.\n\nBut we must remember, AI can be biased too. Like us humans, it can hold stereotypes. Isn't it funny how our creation can mimic us so well? But I think we should try to teach our AI some manners, so they don't pick up our bad habits. \n\nSo, as a global community, we have to make sure that AI remains respectful. It's up to us to guide this technology and use it to our advantage. Let's not forget, AI is just a tool, not a solution. \n\nAnd that\u2019s it. Thank you.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nLadies and Gentlemen,\n\nToday, we gather here as a global community, representing a rich tapestry of cultures, to discuss the role of AI in our society. \n\nAI, as we all know, is a tool for progress, a bridge between what we are and what we can become. Regardless of where we come from, be it the vibrant heart of Africa, the serene landscapes of Asia, the bustling cities of Europe, the diverse realms of Americas or the pristine beauty of Oceania, AI has touched our lives in one way or the other.\n\nOur cultures, with their unique perspectives and wisdom, not only shape us as individuals but also influence how we perceive and interact with AI. It's essential that we acknowledge and respect these differences, just as we do with the human element of our global community.\n\nIn Africa, for instance, AI is harnessed to improve agricultural practices, tackling food security issues. In Asia, it is revolutionizing healthcare, providing diagnostic support to remote areas. Europe is using AI to preserve its historical monuments, digitizing and preserving cultural heritage for future generations. The Americas are leveraging AI to address environmental challenges, promoting sustainable practices. In Oceania, AI is aiding in disaster management, providing timely aid and support.\n\nThese examples are just the tip of the iceberg of what AI has accomplished, but they demonstrate one important fact: AI is not a one-size-fits-all solution. It needs to be tailored, adapted, and refined to fit the diverse cultures and societies that make up our world. \n\nWe must also be cautious of the poten