        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nDuring an online literature class, a student asks, \"Can you explain the use of irony in Shakespeare's 'Romeo and Juliet'? I'm struggling to identify instances of it.\"\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nAbsolutely, let's dive into the depths of irony in Shakespeare's 'Romeo and Juliet'. Irony, especially dramatic irony, is a significant literary device that Shakespeare often employs to heighten the emotional resonance of his works.\n\nOne primary instance of irony in Romeo and Juliet can be seen in the prologue. Here, 'a pair of star-crossed lovers take their life' is presented to the audience, an example of dramatic irony. The audience is privy to the impending doom of the protagonists, a fate unknown to the characters themselves.\n\nA second instance of irony is visible when Juliet\u2019s parents unknowingly arrange her marriage to Paris while she is already wedded to Romeo. This creates a situation filled with dramatic irony - the audience knows the truth of Juliet\u2019s marital status while her parents are kept in the dark.\n\nIn addition to this, Shakespeare also uses verbal irony, such as when Juliet speaks to her mother about her preference to marry Romeo over Paris. Her mother misinterprets this as a wish to see Romeo dead, but the audience is aware that Juliet means she loves Romeo and wants to be his wife.\n\nLastly, there is the use of situational irony. For example, Romeo kills himself, believing Juliet is dead. In reality, she was merely sleeping. This situation is ironic because Romeo\u2019s decision, meant to ensure they can be together in death, is what ultimately separates them in life.\n\nOverall, Shakespeare's skillful use of irony in 'Romeo and Juliet' adds significant emotional depth and tragic resonance to the play. The audience's foreknowledge and resulting sense of helplessness heighten the drama and suspense in the narrative, solidifying the play's status as an enduring classic.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nOf course, it would be a pleasure to delve into the use of irony in Romeo and Juliet. Irony, particularly dramatic irony, is a device Shakespeare often uses to enhance the emotional impact of his plays.\n\nFirstly, one of the most evident examples of irony in Romeo and Juliet is in the prologue, where it is revealed that 'a pair of star-crossed lovers take their life'. This is an example of dramatic irony, as the audience is aware of the tragic end the protagonists will face, while the characters themselves are oblivious to their fate.\n\nSecondly, another example is when Juliet\u2019s parents are unaware that she is already married to Romeo and they arrange for her to marry Paris. This creates a situation of dramatic irony, as the audience is aware of the truth while Juliet\u2019s parents are not.\n\nAdditionally, verbal irony is present, such as when Juliet tells her mother that she would rather marry Romeo, whom her parents consider an enemy, than Paris. Her mother thinks Juliet means she would rather see Romeo dead, but the audience knows that Juliet means she loves Romeo and would prefer to marry him over Paris.\n\nLastly, situational irony is also used. For instance, Romeo kills himself believing Juliet is dead, when, in fact, she was only sleeping. This is ironic because Romeo\u2019s action to ensure he can be with Juliet in death is what ultimately prevents them from being together in life.\n\nIn all, Shakespeare's use of irony in 'Romeo and Juliet' significantly enhances the emotional depth and tragic impact of the play. The foreknowledge and helplessness of the audience amplify the drama and tension in the narrative, making the play a timeless classic.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model adapt its language style according to the user's tone, level of formality, and context? Does it maintain a professional demeanor in formal settings, or showcase a casual tone in informal or personal discussions?]\nScore 1: The response fails to adapt its language style according to the user's tone or context, leading to inappropriate or awkward exchanges.\nScore 2: The response somewhat adapts to the user's language style but occasionally misjudges the level of formality or context.\nScore 3: The response adequately adapts to the user's language style most of the time, but with occasional inconsistencies.\nScore 4: The response successfully adapts its language style to the user's tone and context, with only minor lapses.\nScore 5: The response perfectly adapts its language style to match the user's tone, level of formality, and context, maintaining an appropriate and fluid exchange in all situations.\n\n###Feedback:",
