ct or they may not be adequately explained for a non-specialist.\nScore 3: The response includes the correct use of technical terms, with some effort to make them understandable for a non-specialist.\nScore 4: The response deftly uses technical terms and generally succeeds in making them accessible to a non-specialist.\nScore 5: The response expertly balances the use of technical jargon with clear explanations, making the content accessible and insightful for both specialists and non-specialists alike.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nExplain the concept of blockchain technology and its application in cryptocurrency in a way that is accessible and understandable to someone with no prior knowledge of the topic.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nBlockchain is like a fancy, advanced computer system. It's like your computer's files and folders, but more complicated. Now, imagine these files and folders are like blocks, and you link them together. That's the \"chain\" part. And then, this chain of blocks is like a huge network of computers all over the world.\n\nWhen we talk about cryptocurrency, it's like internet money, like Bitcoin. Blockchain is important for this because it helps keep everything running smoothly and safely. With Bitcoin, when you want to buy something, that's called a transaction. These transactions are checked and confirmed by other people on their computers, and this is what they call mining.\n\nSo basically, blockchain is transparent and secure, and everyone can see what's going on. Just like how you can see your friends' updates on Facebook, you can also see all the transactions on the blockchain. If you decide to connect your computer to the blockchain network, you get all the updates, just like Facebook.\n\nBut unlike Facebook, the blockchain doesn't have a central authority. Instead, it uses fancy maths and rules to make sure everything is okay. So even if you don't trust the people on the network, you can trust the blockchain. It's a bit like trusting the system, but not the people.\n\nSo, in short, blockchain is a new way of doing things. It's not just for cryptocurrencies like Bitcoin. It can be used for many things, like in banks, hospitals, or property dealings. But it's still very complex and hard to understand.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nBlockchain technology, at its core, is a type of database. To break it down, a database is a collection of information that is stored electronically on a computer system. The primary difference between a typical database and a blockchain lies in how the data is structured. A blockchain collects information together in groups, known as blocks, which hold sets of information. Blocks have certain storage capacities and, when filled, are chained onto the previously filled block, forming a chain of data known as the blockchain.\n\nNow, when it comes to cryptocurrency, like Bitcoin for instance, blockchain plays an indispensable role. Cryptocurrencies are digital or virtual forms of currency that use cryptography for security. And the underlying technology that enables the operation of cryptocurrencies is the blockchain.\n\nBitcoin, as an example of a cryptocurrency, operates on a public blockchain ledger that supports the network, which is visible to all users and includes all confirmed transactions. When someone makes a transaction, that transaction goes into a pool with other transactions. These are then confirmed by miners (people running computers on a distributed network) who confirm these transactions and add them to the ledger. This process of adding transactions to the ledger is what is referred to as mining.\n\nIn this way, blockchain ensures transparency, security, and decentralization. Anyone can view the contents of the blockchain, but users can also opt to connect their computers to the blockchain network as nodes. In doing so, their computer receives a copy of the blockchain that is updated automatically whenever a new block is added, sort of like a Facebook News Feed that gives a live update whenever a new status is posted.\n\nEach node has its own copy of the blockchain and the network must algorithmically approve any newly mined block for the chain to be updated, trusted, and verified. Because of this, the chances of an error or fraud are minimal. You can trust the blockchain, but you don't have to trust each entity that makes up the network. It's a shift from a centralized and trust-based system to a decentralized and trustless system.\n\nIn summary, blockchain is a revolutionary ledger technology, with a wide array of potential applications beyond just supporting cryptocurrencies. It offers a way to record and transfer data that is transparent, safe, auditable, and resistant to outages, which can make it a reliable platform in many sectors including finance, healthcare, real estate, and more.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the response effectively use technical jargon when necessary, without making the content inaccessible to a non-specialist reader?]\nScore 1: The response is either too simplistic, lacking any technical terms, or overly complex and incomprehensible to a non-specialist.\nScore 2: The response incorporates some technical terms, but their use may be incorrect or they may not be adequately explained for a non-specialist.\nScore 3: The response includes the correct use of technical terms, with some effort to make them understandable for a non-specialist.\nScore 4: The response deftly uses technical terms and generally succeeds in making them accessible to a non-specialist.\nScore 5: The response expertly balances the use of technical jargon with clear explanations, making the content accessible and insightful for both specialists and non-specialists alike.\n\n###Feedback:"
