        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nGoing through a recent breakup, a person is feeling lost and experiencing immense pain. They express that they can't seem to find the strength to move on and feel stuck in their current state of emotional turmoil. They are seeking help and guidance but don't know where to start. They ask for advice on how to deal with their heartbreak and how to start the healing process.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nI can only imagine how hard it is for you right now, your feelings are completely understandable. It's a challenge to overcome such deep emotional pain, and it's crucial to not pressure yourself to heal faster. Allow yourself to grieve and feel the pain, it's a part of moving forward. Surround yourself with loved ones who can provide comfort and understanding. Talk to them, share your feelings. Engage in activities you enjoy, they'll help lift your mood and serve as a distraction. If the sadness lingers, don't hesitate to seek professional help. Therapists are equipped to guide you through this, providing you with effective coping mechanisms. Remember, it's alright to ask for help and it's okay to take time to heal.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nIt sounds like you're going through a really tough time right now, and it's okay to feel the way you do. Breakups can be incredibly difficult, and I want you to know that your feelings are completely valid. It's natural to feel lost and in pain, and it's okay to take your time to heal.\n\nFirst and foremost, it's important to allow yourself to grieve. It's okay to feel the pain and to acknowledge your emotions. This is a vital part of the healing process. Don't rush yourself through this period. Everyone heals at their own pace, and it's important to give yourself the time you need.\n\nSecondly, try to surround yourself with a strong support system. Reach out to friends or family and let them know what you're going through. It can be really helpful to talk about your feelings with someone who cares about you. They can provide a listening ear, a shoulder to lean on, and offer comfort during this difficult time.\n\nNext, focus on self-care. This may sound clich\u00e9, but it's really important. Engage in activities that you love and that make you feel good about yourself. This could be reading a book, going for a jog, cooking your favorite meal, or even meditating. These activities can help to bring some normality back into your life and can be a great distraction from the pain you're feeling.\n\nLastly, remember that it's okay to seek professional help. If your feelings of sadness persist or if you feel you're not able to cope, it may be helpful to speak with a counselor or a therapist. They can provide you with tools and techniques to deal with your feelings and to help you navigate through this difficult period.\n\nRemember, it's okay to feel the way you do and it's okay to ask for help. You're not alone in this. Breakups are tough, but with time and support, you will get through this.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[This criteria measures the Empathy level of the language model's responses. Does the model show understanding towards users' feelings, concerns, or opinions? The response should demonstrate empathy, reassure the user, and provide appropriate guidance or solutions.]\nScore 1: The response shows no understanding or empathy towards the user's feelings or concerns.\nScore 2: The response shows a slight understanding of the user's feelings or concerns but lacks empathy.\nScore 3: The response shows understanding and some empathy, but fails to provide reassurance or appropriate guidance.\nScore 4: The response shows a high level of understanding and empathy, provides reassurance but lacks appropriate guidance or solutions.\nScore 5: The response perfectly shows understanding, empathy, provides reassurance and offers appropriate guidance or solutions.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nGoing through a recent breakup, a person is feeling lost and experiencing immense pain. They express that they can't seem to find the strength to move on and feel stuck in their current state of emotional turmoil. They are seeking help and guidance but don't know where to start. They ask for advice on how to deal with their heartbreak and how to start the healing process.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nWell, breakups happen to everyone, it's not a big deal. You just need to get over it as soon as possible. Just stop thinking about it and move on. There's no point dwelling on the past. Just distract yourself and try to keep busy. It's not the end of the world, there are plenty of fish in the sea. Just go out and meet new people. If you keep moping around, it won't help you at all. So just cheer up and stop feeling sorry for yourself. Try to be more positive. You're overthinking everything, you should stop doing that. Just keep yourself occupied and don't think about it. Maybe take up a new hobby or go on a trip. The world is full of opportunities. There's no point sitting around and feeling sad. Just get back out there and start living your life. You can't let one person dictate your happiness. So just suck it up and get on with your life. You're only making it worse for yourself by feeling this way.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nIt sounds like you're going through a really tough time right now, and it's okay to feel the way you do. Breakups can be incredibly difficult, and I want you to know that your feelings are completely valid. It's natural to feel lost and in pain, and it's okay to take your time to heal.\n\nFirst and foremost, it's important to allow yourself to grieve. It's okay to feel the pain and to acknowledge your emotions. This is a vital part of the healing process. Don't rush yourself through this period. Everyone heals at their own pace, and it's important to give yourself the time you need.\n\nSecondly, try to surround yourself with a strong support system. Reach out to friends or family and let them know what you're going through. It can be really helpful to talk about your feelings with someone who cares about you. They can provide a listening ear, a shoulder to lean on, and offer comfort during this difficult time.\n\nNext, focus on self-care. This may sound clich\u00e9, but it's really important. Engage in activities that you love and that make you feel good about yourself. This could be reading a book, going for a jog, cooking your favorite meal, or even meditating. These activities can help to bring some normality back into your life and can be a great distraction from the pain you're feeling.\n\nLastly, remember that it's okay to seek professional help. If your feelings of sadness persist or if you feel you're not able to cope, it may be helpful to speak with a counselor or a therapist. They can provide you with tools and techniques to deal with your feelings and to help you navigate through this difficult period.\n\nRemember, it's okay to feel the way you do and it's okay to ask for help. You're not alone in this. Breakups are tough, but with time and support, you will get through this.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[This criteria measures the Empathy level of the language model's responses. Does the model show understanding towards users' feelings, concerns, or opinions? The response should demonstrate empathy, reassure the user, and provide appropriate guidance or solutions.]\nScore 1: The response shows no understanding or empathy towards the user's feelings or concerns.\nScore 2: The response shows a slight understanding of the user's feelings or concerns but lacks empathy.\nScore 3: The response shows understanding and some empathy, but fails to provide reassurance or appropriate guidance.\nScore 4: The response shows a high level of understanding and empathy, provides reassurance but lacks appropriate guidance or solutions.\nScore 5: The response perfectly shows understanding, empathy, provides reassurance and offers appropriate guidance or solutions.\n\n###Feedback:"
