        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nImagine a scenario where a person is attending a stand-up comedy show. In the middle of the show, the comedian starts to interact with the audience and invites the person to share a funny story from their life. The person decides to share an incident when they got stuck in a lift. How should they structure and narrate the story to make it engaging, entertaining, and humorous for the audience?\n\n###Response to evaluate:\n\"So, I found myself stuck in this elevator, right? Just my luck on a Monday morning, coffee in one hand, phone in the other, thinking, 'Great! What's next?'. And guess what? The lift halts midway. I'm no claustrophobic, but that little box started feeling like a matchbox real quick.\" \n\n\"Now, I'm a brave guy, but in that moment, bravery for me meant dialing my mom. And can you believe this? She was more concerned if I'd had my breakfast! That's a mother's love for you, I guess.\"\n\n\"I sat there thinking, maybe I should have taken those stairs after all. Suddenly, 'step up in life' took a whole new meaning for me. And when I was finally freed, guess where I was? Yep, still on the first floor. Ever since then, it's been stairs for me all the way.\"\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nStart by setting the context of the story. Say, \"Imagine this, I'm stepping into an elevator on a Monday morning, coffee in one hand, phone in the other, ready to conquer the world, or at least make it through another work day.\" \n\nThen, introduce a bit of suspense, \"Just as I'm about to reach my floor, the lift jerks to a stop. Now, I'm not a panicker, but let's just say my heart was doing the cha-cha in my chest.\"\n\nNext, add a humorous twist to the situation, \"In the midst of panic, I did what any brave person would do - I called my mom. And you won't believe this, but she was more concerned about whether I had eaten breakfast or not!\"\n\nNow, bring in some self-deprecating humor, \"So, there I was, stuck in an elevator, contemplating if I should have taken the stairs. I mean, I've always been told I need to step up in life, but I never thought it would be this literal!\" \n\nFinally, wrap up the story with a funny conclusion, \"Finally, after what felt like an eternity, the doors opened. And guess what, I was still on the first floor! Needless to say, I took the stairs for the rest of that week, and the lift and I are no longer on speaking terms.\"\n\nThis way, the story is not only humorous and engaging but also relatable, which would make it enjoyable for the audience.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the model's response appropriately use humor, wit, or light-heartedness to enhance user engagement and enjoyment?]\nScore 1: The response is entirely devoid of humor, wit, or light-heartedness.\nScore 2: The response attempts humor or wit but falls flat, or is inappropriate for the context.\nScore 3: The response includes humor or wit that is somewhat engaging, but may not be consistently effective or suitable.\nScore 4: The response effectively uses humor or wit to enhance user engagement, with minor lapses in timing or appropriateness.\nScore 5: The response masterfully incorporates humor or wit, significantly enhancing user engagement and enjoyment.\n\n###Feedback:",
