        "chosen_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nImagine a situation where an individual is having a bad day and decides to share their feelings through written text. The person expresses their disappointment, sadness, and frustration in different sentences, using different words and expressions. The task is to respond to this individual's remarks in a way that shows understanding and empathy to the individual's feelings, adjusting the language and tone based on the emotions conveyed.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nI can understand why you're feeling this way. It sounds like you're going through a tough time. It's completely okay to feel disappointed and frustrated, we all have such moments. You may find it helpful to take some time for yourself, maybe indulge in activities you love. If you're comfortable, consider sharing your feelings with a trusted individual. And remember, it's okay to have bad days, they're a part of life. Don't let them define you. I'm here for you.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nI'm really sorry to hear that you're having such a rough day. It sounds like you're dealing with a lot right now, and it's completely okay to feel disappointed and frustrated. We all have those days when nothing seems to go right, and it can be tough to see any positivity. \n\nPlease remember, it's perfectly okay to take a step back and give yourself the time you need to process these feelings. Sometimes, a bit of self-care can make a big difference. You might find it helpful to do something you enjoy, even if it's just for a few minutes. Listening to your favorite music, reading a good book, or even taking a walk can sometimes help shift your perspective. \n\nIt's also important to remember that it's okay to ask for help. If you have someone you trust, consider sharing your feelings with them. They might be able to offer you some comfort or advice. And if your feelings of sadness persist, it might be a good idea to speak to a professional who can provide you with some further guidance. \n\nI hope things start looking up for you soon. Remember, it's okay to have bad days. They're part of life, and they don't define you. Hang in there.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the response adapt its language and tone based on the user's input? Consideration should be given to whether the response is able to pick up on the user's emotions and adjust its language accordingly.]\nScore 1: The response does not recognise the user's emotions at all and uses a uniform language and tone across all responses.\nScore 2: The response slightly adapts its language and tone based on the user's emotions but in a very limited and inconsistent way.\nScore 3: The response does a fair job at adapting its language and tone based on the user's emotions but could still improve in terms of consistency and range of emotions recognised.\nScore 4: The response consistently adapts its language and tone based on the user's emotions but may still miss out on a few subtle cues.\nScore 5: The response perfectly recognises the user's emotions and adjusts its language and tone accordingly in a seamless and natural manner.\n\n###Feedback:",
        "rejected_instruction": "###Task Description:\nAn instruction (might include an Input inside it), a response to evaluate, a reference answer that gets a score of 5, and a score rubric representing a evaluation criteria are given.\n1. Write a detailed feedback that assess the quality of the response strictly based on the given score rubric, not evaluating in general.\n2. After writing a feedback, write a score that is an integer between 1 and 5. You should refer to the score rubric.\n3. The output format should look as follows: \"Feedback: (write a feedback for criteria) [RESULT] (an integer number between 1 and 5)\"\n4. Please do not generate any other opening, closing, and explanations.\n\n###The instruction to evaluate:\nImagine a situation where an individual is having a bad day and decides to share their feelings through written text. The person expresses their disappointment, sadness, and frustration in different sentences, using different words and expressions. The task is to respond to this individual's remarks in a way that shows understanding and empathy to the individual's feelings, adjusting the language and tone based on the emotions conveyed.\n\n###Response to evaluate:\nOh, that sounds tough. Sometimes, it's okay to feel sad, so don't beat yourself up too much about it. Try to think of happier times and this feeling will pass soon. Do something you love, it might make you feel better. Do you like listening to music? Or reading a book? These can be good distractions. But don't push away these feelings, acknowledge them, and let them be. Everyone has bad days, and it's normal. You can try sharing your feelings with someone close to you. They might have good advice to offer. Also, talking to a professional might be a good idea if these feelings of sadness persist. Remember, every day is a new day and it's going to be okay. Bad days come, but they also go. You're stronger than you think, and you'll get through this.\n\n###Reference Answer (Score 5):\nI'm really sorry to hear that you're having such a rough day. It sounds like you're dealing with a lot right now, and it's completely okay to feel disappointed and frustrated. We all have those days when nothing seems to go right, and it can be tough to see any positivity. \n\nPlease remember, it's perfectly okay to take a step back and give yourself the time you need to process these feelings. Sometimes, a bit of self-care can make a big difference. You might find it helpful to do something you enjoy, even if it's just for a few minutes. Listening to your favorite music, reading a good book, or even taking a walk can sometimes help shift your perspective. \n\nIt's also important to remember that it's okay to ask for help. If you have someone you trust, consider sharing your feelings with them. They might be able to offer you some comfort or advice. And if your feelings of sadness persist, it might be a good idea to speak to a professional who can provide you with some further guidance. \n\nI hope things start looking up for you soon. Remember, it's okay to have bad days. They're part of life, and they don't define you. Hang in there.\n\n###Score Rubrics:\n[Does the response adapt its language and tone based on the user's input? Consideration should be given to whether the response is able to pick up on the user's emotions and adjust its language accordingly.]\nScore 1: The response does not recognise the user's emotions at all and uses a uniform language and tone across all responses.\nScore 2: The response slightly adapts its language and tone based on the user's emotions but in a very limited and inconsistent way.\nScore 3: The response does a fair job at adapting its language and tone based on the user's emotions but could still improve in terms of consistency and range of emotions recognised.\nScore 4: The response consistently adapts its language and tone based on the user's emotions but may still miss out on a few subtle cues.\nScore 5: The response perfec